#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tex-blame — git blame for LaTeX papers, rendered as a compiled PDF.

Given a chain of revisions of a LaTeX project (a base plus one or more rounds
of edits), tex-blame compiles a single PDF in which:

  * text present in the base revision stays black,
  * text added by each round is set in that round's colour (in the git modes,
    rounds are grouped by commit author, so each author gets a colour),
  * deletions are struck through in the colour of the round that removed them,
  * scaffold macros (\\todo, \\decide, ...) that a round answered are struck
    through in the answerer's colour instead of silently disappearing.

Modes:
  --git BASE R1 [R2 ...]   explicit chain of git revisions (BASE shown black)
  --since BASE             blame every commit after BASE (first-parent chain,
                           consecutive commits by one author squashed into one
                           round); BASE and before shown black
  --all                    blame the whole history: the base is the empty tree,
                           so every word is attributed to an author
  --dirs D0 D1 [D2 ...]    explicit chain of directories (no git needed)

The chain must be linear: each revision is assumed to contain the previous
one's changes (in git terms, the first-parent path).

How it works (see README for details): files changed by only one round get a
plain two-way diff attributed wholly to that round; files changed by several
rounds get a chained diff, newest round first, with each stage's markup moved
into a private per-round namespace so attribution cannot leak. Preamble
material is taken verbatim from the newest revision, everything is flattened
into one .tex, and latexmk compiles it inside the newest tree.

Requires: latexdiff, latexmk (and a TeX distribution), git for the git modes.

